WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:05.000
Peace be upon you and welcome to the fourth video of the second module in our fellowship.

00:05.000 --> 00:10.000
To understand one of the most important issues in the world of public health, let us imagine a small story:

00:10.000 --> 00:17.000
Imagine an intense football match is underway, and three people are standing outside wanting to watch the match, dying to know the score.

00:17.000 --> 00:21.000
Yet unfortunately, in front of them is a very high wooden fence blocking their view.

00:21.000 --> 00:24.000
Before we continue the story, let us go to our question today:

00:24.000 --> 00:29.000
If we give all people the exact same thing, have we achieved justice in that way?

00:29.000 --> 00:36.000
Meaning, if the three have different heights: one tall, the second medium, and the third short, and a good person wanted to help them.

00:36.000 --> 00:40.000
So he brought three wooden boxes identical to the millimeter and gave each one a box to stand on.

00:40.000 --> 00:45.000
From the outside, you would say: "Look at what equality and justice! Everyone took the exact same opportunity."

00:45.000 --> 00:48.000
Yet was the result truly just?

00:48.000 --> 00:50.000
Let us see the reality of what occurred:

00:50.000 --> 00:52.000
The tall person, who could already see the pitch before,

00:52.000 --> 00:56.000
now sees the ball, the referee's corner, and the penalty kick with double clarity.

00:56.000 --> 01:00.000
The medium person: the box barely helped him, and now he sees the match properly.

01:00.000 --> 01:06.000
As for the short person, even with the box under his feet, the fence remains higher than his eyes, and he still sees only wood and nails.

01:06.000 --> 01:14.000
And right here, this famous illustration illustrates the profound difference between two concepts that many confuse:

01:14.000 --> 01:19.000
Equality versus health equity.

01:19.000 --> 01:24.000
In this journey, we will deconstruct this difference and its direct impact on vulnerable groups.

01:24.000 --> 01:32.000
The idea of equality is simply to distribute the same resources and services to everyone without looking at where they start from or what their circumstances are.

01:32.000 --> 01:38.000
Imagine, for example, printing a luxurious awareness brochure written in complex formal Arabic and distributing it equally to all city residents:

01:38.000 --> 01:42.000
Have we achieved full equality in this way? Yes. But is this just? Certainly not.

01:42.000 --> 01:44.000
Because the educated person will read and benefit,

01:44.000 --> 01:50.000
whereas for someone who cannot read or whose eyesight is poor, the brochure for them is barely paper to wrap a falafel sandwich in.

01:50.000 --> 01:54.000
They fell completely outside the circle of benefit even though we equalized the giving.

01:54.000 --> 01:57.000
Here comes the higher concept: health equity.

01:57.000 --> 01:59.000
Equity is not giving everyone the same performance;

01:59.000 --> 02:04.000
equity is giving each person precisely what they need to reach the best possible health level.

02:04.000 --> 02:05.000
Let us return to the fence story:

02:05.000 --> 02:08.000
Equity here is giving the short person two boxes,

02:08.000 --> 02:11.000
and taking the box from the tall person who did not need it in the first place.

02:11.000 --> 02:17.000
Equity is an explicit recognition that there are people who need additional support to overcome barriers and reach safety.

02:17.000 --> 02:20.000
Let us apply this to a living example we see often:

02:20.000 --> 02:24.000
Imagine a health team conducted an excellent awareness campaign for heatwave prevention:

02:24.000 --> 02:29.000
wonderful videos, motion graphics, and designs, and published them all on social media.

02:29.000 --> 02:32.000
On the surface, the campaign is free and accessible to everyone.

02:32.000 --> 02:34.000
Yet did protection reach everyone?

02:34.000 --> 02:39.000
Youth with smartphones and internet benefited greatly, while entire segments fell out of our calculations:

02:39.000 --> 02:44.000
Our grandparents, elderly people with no grasp of technology whose phones are simple Nokia devices with a flashlight;

02:44.000 --> 02:50.000
the visually impaired; and our people in refugee camps and remote villages where electricity cuts off for hours and where there is no network in the first place.

02:50.000 --> 02:55.000
Crafting a single message and sending it through one channel never meant you protected everyone.

02:55.000 --> 03:00.000
This takes us to a fundamental term in public health: groups most at risk.

03:00.000 --> 03:04.000
When we say that, we do not mean that they are weak by nature; not at all.

03:04.000 --> 03:12.000
We mean that their environmental and material circumstances and the nature of their bodies place them in the face of the storm, exposed to risk more than others.

03:12.000 --> 03:17.000
Children: their immunity and organs are still growing, and they lose fluids faster than adults.

03:17.000 --> 03:22.000
Their ability to adapt to heat decreases; most have chronic heart and blood pressure illnesses.

03:22.000 --> 03:30.000
Pregnant women undergo double hormonal and physiological changes, requiring utmost care with hydration and rest for the fetal heartbeat.

03:30.000 --> 03:36.000
Persons with disabilities face architectural and informational barriers that hinder their access to care or even warnings.

03:36.000 --> 03:43.000
Refugees and displaced persons living in tents and corrugated tin shacks that lack insulation, transforming under the sun into a thermal oven.

03:43.000 --> 03:48.000
Low-income earners whose conditions prevent them from buying protective supplies or even paying hospital transit fares.

03:48.000 --> 03:52.000
And if you want to see health equity clearly, consider severe heatwaves:

03:52.000 --> 03:57.000
The well-off person sits in an insulated house with air conditioning running and a refrigerator full of cold water;

03:57.000 --> 04:03.000
compare them with the construction or agricultural laborer working under a sun scorching stone to earn his children's livelihood,

04:03.000 --> 04:07.000
or a family in an informal settlement whose electricity is cut off and whose water is scarce.

04:07.000 --> 04:11.000
The climate danger is one, yet the bodily blow and suffering are not equal at all.

04:11.000 --> 04:13.000
What does this mean for the health educator?

04:13.000 --> 04:18.000
This places in our hands the compass of equity, composed of four questions to ask before any initiative:

04:18.000 --> 04:20.000
Who is the actual target audience?

04:20.000 --> 04:24.000
Who are the vulnerable groups needing an additional box of support?

04:24.000 --> 04:28.000
Was the message formulated in a way that matches everyone's capabilities?

04:28.000 --> 04:31.000
And what are the barriers that might prevent people from benefiting and applying?

04:31.000 --> 04:34.000
To apply this practically in a dehydration prevention campaign:

04:34.000 --> 04:37.000
For youth: fast, interactive digital content on social media.

04:37.000 --> 04:43.000
For the elderly: we go to community centers for warm face-to-face sessions and speak in their language.

04:43.000 --> 04:49.000
And for people with limited literacy: simple posters with symbols and illustrations that are understood without many words.

04:49.000 --> 04:53.000
In conclusion: Equality is giving everyone the same thing and easing your conscience on paper.

04:53.000 --> 05:00.000
While health equity is giving each person exactly what they need to ensure you protected the people in greatest need.

05:00.000 --> 05:05.000
In our next exploration, we will move to a strategic question that will change the way you think:

05:05.000 --> 05:08.000
Do we stay sitting in the hospital waiting for patients to treat them?

05:08.000 --> 05:11.000
Or do we go to the head of the river and prevent them from falling in the first place?

05:11.000 --> 05:15.000
This is what we will learn when we discuss prevention before cure.

05:15.000 --> 05:21.000
Before concluding, think with us: choose a group in your community, consider their greatest challenge in the summer,

05:21.000 --> 05:28.000
how climate change worsens their plight, and most importantly, what extra box you can offer them.

05:28.000 --> 05:32.000
Thank you for your attention, and we will see you in the next video, God willing.
